WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.735
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:15.054
Unit six: Advanced Negotiation. Today you will highlight what matters most with cleft sentences.

4
00:00:15.354 --> 00:00:17.855
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.777
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:23.699
Leverage.

7
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:28.506
Leverage means an advantage that gives you power to influence an outcome.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:32.544
Exclusive data gave the start-up considerable leverage.

9
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.489
Your turn. Say: Leverage.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.369
Leverage.

11
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:41.222
Compromise.

12
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:45.493
Compromise means an agreement reached by mutual concession.

13
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:49.360
The final compromise satisfied neither side completely.

14
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.591
Your turn. Say: Compromise.

15
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:56.458
Compromise.

16
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:58.078
Win-win.

17
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:02.139
Win-win means beneficial to all parties involved.

18
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.783
Skilled negotiators look for win-win outcomes.

19
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:08.653
Your turn. Say: Win-win.

20
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.757
Win-win.

21
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:14.553
Mediation.

22
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:19.256
Mediation means intervention by a neutral party to resolve a dispute.

23
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.720
Mediation succeeded where direct talks had failed.

24
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:25.911
Your turn. Say: Mediation.

25
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:29.884
Mediation.

26
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:31.953
Bargaining power.

27
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.631
Bargaining power means the relative ability of each side to influence terms.

28
00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:40.506
Unions increase workers' bargaining power.

29
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.792
Your turn. Say: Bargaining power.

30
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:48.134
Bargaining power.

31
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:49.861
Concession.

32
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.545
Concession means something granted to the other side to reach agreement.

33
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.742
Neither party was willing to make the first concession.

34
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.921
Your turn. Say: Concession.

35
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:04.849
Concession.

36
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:06.642
Strategic.

37
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.938
Strategic means planned to achieve a long-term objective.

38
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.898
Her silence was a strategic choice.

39
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:17.519
Your turn. Say: Strategic.

40
00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.286
Strategic.

41
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.028
Impasse.

42
00:02:24.017 --> 00:02:27.398
Impasse means a deadlock in which no progress is possible.

43
00:02:27.983 --> 00:02:30.668
Talks reached an impasse over working hours.

44
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:33.959
Your turn. Say: Impasse.

45
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.952
Impasse.

46
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:39.962
Arbitration.

47
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:46.502
Arbitration means settlement of a dispute by an impartial third party whose decision is binding.

48
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:50.521
The contract requires arbitration in case of disagreement.

49
00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:53.897
Your turn. Say: Arbitration.

50
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:57.781
Arbitration.

51
00:02:58.517 --> 00:02:59.576
Stalemate.

52
00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:04.487
Stalemate means a situation in which neither side can make progress.

53
00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:07.745
The dispute ended in a costly stalemate.

54
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.666
Your turn. Say: Stalemate.

55
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:14.687
Stalemate.

56
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.406
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:26.722
Visal, your team negotiated the school's new partnership with the tech company. How did you secure such good terms?

58
00:03:27.817 --> 00:03:37.191
It is leverage that determines negotiating power. We had something they wanted: access to three hundred enthusiastic student testers.

59
00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:40.062
Did the talks go smoothly?

60
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:49.180
Not at all. We reached an impasse over data privacy. What broke the deadlock was a strategic concession on the timeline.

61
00:03:50.283 --> 00:03:52.434
Was mediation ever considered?

62
00:03:53.550 --> 00:04:01.775
Briefly. But it was the principal who suggested a compromise, and both sides accepted it as a genuine win-win.

63
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:04.410
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.818
Grammar focus: Cleft sentences.

65
00:04:08.118 --> 00:04:09.352
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.176
It is leverage that determines negotiating power.

67
00:04:14.083 --> 00:04:16.814
What broke the deadlock was a strategic concession.

68
00:04:17.717 --> 00:04:20.471
It was the principal who suggested a compromise.

69
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:28.985
Cleft sentences divide a message into two clauses to spotlight one element. Use 'who' for people and 'that' for things after 'It is / was …'.

70
00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:33.900
Be careful! Don't say: It was the principal which suggested it.

71
00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:38.226
Say: It was the principal who suggested it.

72
00:04:38.526 --> 00:04:41.037
Use 'who' for people in a cleft sentence.

73
00:04:41.833 --> 00:04:44.312
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.867
Inside the Negotiating Room.

75
00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:51.744
Successful negotiation is rarely about who shouts loudest or concedes least.

76
00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:58.750
It is leverage that determines negotiating power, and leverage depends on what the other side needs.

77
00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:05.309
When talks reach an impasse, what often breaks the deadlock is a small but strategic concession.

78
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:12.990
If direct talks fail, mediation offers a neutral perspective, while arbitration imposes a binding decision.

79
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.821
The most durable agreements are win-win compromises in which neither party feels defeated.

80
00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:24.016
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.755
It was the principal blank suggested a compromise.

82
00:05:28.055 --> 00:05:31.209
A, which. B, what. C, who.

83
00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.720
The answer is C: who.

84
00:05:37.883 --> 00:05:41.325
Rewrite with emphasis: 'A concession broke the deadlock.'

85
00:05:41.625 --> 00:05:48.924
A, What broke the deadlock was a concession.. B, What did break the deadlock a concession.. C, It a concession broke the deadlock..

86
00:05:52.467 --> 00:05:55.801
The answer is A: What broke the deadlock was a concession..

87
00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.401
Which process results in a binding decision by a third party?

88
00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:05.248
A, mediation. B, arbitration. C, bargaining.

89
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.032
The answer is B: arbitration.

90
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:14.903
Talks that are completely blocked have reached an blank.

91
00:06:15.203 --> 00:06:19.170
A, impasse. B, leverage. C, concession.

92
00:06:22.733 --> 00:06:24.756
The answer is A: impasse.

93
00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:28.717
According to the reading, leverage depends on blank.

94
00:06:29.017 --> 00:06:34.853
A, how loudly you speak. B, what the other side needs. C, the number of concessions.

95
00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.375
The answer is B: what the other side needs.

96
00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:45.393
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:49.596
What negotiation strategy do you find most effective?

98
00:07:06.383 --> 00:07:07.802
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:08.102 --> 00:07:40.496
It is active listening that I find most effective in negotiation. When you understand what the other side truly needs, you can identify concessions that cost you little but matter greatly to them. For instance, in a group project I once accepted a later deadline in exchange for choosing our topic. Some people prefer an aggressive strategy, and it can work in one-off deals. However, in long-term relationships, a win-win approach builds trust and lasting results.

100
00:07:40.796 --> 00:07:42.795
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:43.683 --> 00:07:47.601
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and cleft sentences.

102
00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:52.237
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

103
00:07:52.537 --> 00:07:54.957
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

104
00:07:55.257 --> 00:07:57.967
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

